# The Elm architecture's contract, and nothing else: a model type `M` names its
# message type, names what its view is, and provides the two functions the loop
# needs. The driver loop itself lives in a vocabulary's package, because a
# driver has to paint, and painting is the one thing core cannot do.
#
# `update`'s row is `!M.E`, an associated effect with a pure default. That is
# the shape a per-app row has to take: a method's own effect variable belongs
# to the *caller*, so an effect the implementation decides needs a member of
# the trait to be named by (docs/effect-params-design.md 刀 5). The member
# defaults to `!()` (#369), so an impl that says nothing gets a loop exactly
# as pure as before, `==` still tests a turn, and nothing crosses a boundary
# that did not cross it before -- a row of `!()` is an empty evidence pack.
# What the member buys is that an app which does need io binds its own row
# (`effect E = !io`) without forcing it on every other app, which is what an io
# `Cmd` is going to want.
#
# The `Cmd` channel itself is here now: `update` answers `(M, Cmd[M.Msg])` and
# `tea_core/cmd` folds what the command asked for into the same turn. Its arms
# are the ones a pure fold can discharge, so an io command -- one whose answer
# arrives after the reply has been written -- is still missing, and so is the
# wire op a driver would need to feed that answer back in. Those two are step
# two, together (docs/oneshot-design.md §6.1 and its verdict ⑥).
#
# `packages/tea-dom`'s reactor is deliberately not part of this. It takes
# `update` as an ordinary function parameter rather than through the trait, its
# `turn` is pure so that tests can assert with `==`, and its failure barrier is
# pinned `!io` by the language. Reconciling those three with a row the app
# decides is a design judgement, not a signature edit, and it is deferred to
# tea v2's io commands along with the wire op they need.
#
# `type View` is bare, and it has to be: an associated type cannot declare
# bounds ("`type View` is the whole declaration; the impl chooses the type"), so
# there is no way to write `type View: Tree`. The consequence is bigger than the
# missing bound. A generic function over `A: App` can do nothing with an
# `A.View` but return it -- a bound applies to a type parameter, and `A.View` is
# not one -- and pinning the tree type as `Widget[A.Msg]` at the driver does not
# rescue it either, because the reconciler wants `Eq` on the tree, structural
# `Eq` on the tree wants `Eq` on the message, and `A.Msg` is an associated type
# that cannot carry that bound either.
#
# So a driver that reconciles takes the view as an ordinary function parameter
# over its own bounded tree type: `fn drive[A: App, W: Tree + Eq](m: A, vw:
# fn(A) -> W, ...)`. What that costs, stated plainly rather than hidden: nothing
# ties `W` back to `A.View` any more, so such a signature accepts a tree over
# the wrong message type. `type View` stays here as the documented truth --
# `dawn doc` renders it, and a reader can see that the `W` a driver takes is
# meant to be it -- but the machine no longer checks the tie. This is the one
# place the split loses type safety, and the language has no way to buy it back
# short of bounds or equality constraints on associated types, neither of which
# exists.

use cmd.{Cmd, NoCmd, SendMsg, BatchCmd, fold_msg}

## An application: `update` folds one message into the model and says what
## else the turn asks for, `view` describes the whole screen for a model. Both
## pure, so both are testable with `==`.
pub trait App[M] {
  type Msg
  type View
  effect E = !()
  fn update(m: M, msg: M.Msg) -> (M, Cmd[M.Msg]) !M.E
  fn view(m: M) -> M.View
}

# A minimal app for the test blocks below; real apps bring their own. Its view
# is a `String` on purpose. Core must work for any view type at all, and a
# widget tree here would be exactly the assumption this package exists to
# avoid; tea_term/step is where the same trait meets a real tree.

type Counter = { n: Int }

type CounterMsg =
  | Inc
  | Dec
  | Twice
derive Show

impl App[Counter] {
  type Msg = CounterMsg
  type View = String
  fn update(m: Counter, msg: CounterMsg) -> (Counter, Cmd[CounterMsg]) =
    match msg {
      Inc -> (Counter { n: m.n + 1 }, NoCmd)
      Dec -> (Counter { n: m.n - 1 }, NoCmd)
      # Two steps in one message, said as a command rather than by calling
      # `update` from inside itself: the fold is what runs the second one.
      Twice -> (m, BatchCmd(cmds: [SendMsg(msg: Inc), SendMsg(msg: Inc)]))
    }
  fn view(m: Counter) -> String = "n=" ++ to_string(m.n)
}

# What a driver does with a turn, written once for the assertions below.
fn turn(m: Counter, msg: CounterMsg) -> Counter = fold_msg(m, msg, (a, x) => update(a, x))

test "update folds messages, view snapshots as a value" {
  let m = turn(turn(Counter { n: 0 }, Inc), Inc)
  assert m == Counter { n: 2 }
  assert view(turn(m, Dec)) == "n=1"
}

test "a turn is the update and every message its command asked for" {
  # `Twice` moves no field itself; the model that comes back is the fold's.
  assert turn(Counter { n: 0 }, Twice) == Counter { n: 2 }
  # And the raw pair still says what was commanded, which is what a driver
  # other than this one reads.
  let (kept, c) = update(Counter { n: 0 }, Twice)
  assert kept == Counter { n: 0 }
  assert c == BatchCmd(cmds: [SendMsg(msg: Inc), SendMsg(msg: Inc)])
}

test "the view type is whatever the impl says, core never assumes a tree" {
  assert view(Counter { n: 7 }) == "n=7"
}
